      correctAnswer: "A",
      explanation: "**Choice A is correct.** The real subject of the verb is the singular \"row\" (\"The row of rubber rollers that carries the fruit\"), so it takes the singular \"turns.\"\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nThe rule: a verb must match the main noun of its subject (singular or plural), not the nearest word.\n- How to spot it: find the ONE word the sentence is really about and cut the words in between. Strip \"of rubber rollers that carries the fruit past the graders\" and you are left with \"The row ... turns\" - singular.\n- The main noun is the singular \"row,\" which needs the singular \"turns.\"\n- The plural \"rollers\" sits in between and does not control the verb.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- B: \"Turn\" is the plural form and does not match the singular \"row.\"\n- C: \"Have turned\" is plural (it would need \"has\") and shifts the time frame for no reason.\n- D: \"Are turning\" is plural and clashes with the singular \"row.\""
