      correctAnswer: "B",
      explanation: "**Choice B is correct.** The sentence lists three vibration patterns, and each item already carries commas inside it (\"the whole head rising and falling together, which gives ...\"). When list items are long and already contain commas, semicolons separate the items; the semicolon after \"tone\" makes the first break.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nThe rule: use semicolons between list items when the items themselves already contain commas.\n- How to spot it: look at the list. Each item here carries its own \"which\" description with a comma, so the dividers between items have to move up to semicolons.\n- The later items are already separated by a semicolon, which confirms the pattern the list is using.\n- A semicolon after \"tone\" cleanly divides the first item from the second.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: A comma blends in with the commas already inside the items, blurring where each item ends.\n- C: With no punctuation, the first two items run straight together.\n- D: It drops \"and\" into the middle of a series that is separated by semicolons and closes with \"and\" before the final item."
